-- `Debug.log` follows the EDGE and not the declaration
-- (`docs/design/backend.md` §9's "Purity, and what may be dropped").
--
-- §9 drops an unreachable declaration entire, initialiser included, and the
-- rule is total because the premise is: a top-level beni value is pure to
-- evaluate. `Debug.log` is the one deliberate violation of that in the
-- language (`boundary.md` §4) — and it is a FUNCTION, so its effect happens
-- when it is CALLED, and a call is an edge. A `Debug.log` reachable from
-- `main` keeps everything it names; a `Debug.log` in a declaration nothing
-- reaches is deleted with it and never runs.
--
-- So: exactly one line of log output, from `reached`. Two other
-- declarations could add one and neither does:
--
--   - `neverCalled` is `pub`, is a perfectly good call of `Debug.log`, and
--     prints nothing, because nothing calls it;
--   - `neverEvaluated` is a top-level CONSTANT whose initialiser calls
--     `Debug.log`. It is the sharp one: a constant's initialiser runs at
--     module evaluation time, so a pass that kept the declaration — or that
--     kept the declaration and dropped only its body — would print a second
--     line before `main` ran. §9 drops it entire, initialiser included.
--
-- Three lines would mean the pass kept a declaration it should not have;
-- zero would mean it dropped one it should have kept, and the arithmetic
-- below would be missing too.
--
-- `Debug.log` takes the value first and the label second and returns the
-- value unchanged, so the logged line and the printed line come from one
-- expression.
import Node exposing (Program)
import String


reached : Int -> Int
reached n =
    Debug.log n "reached" * 2


pub neverCalled : Int -> Int
neverCalled n =
    Debug.log n "neverCalled" + 1


pub neverEvaluated : Int
neverEvaluated =
    Debug.log 1 "neverEvaluated" + 1


main : Program
main =
    Node.printLines [ String.fromInt (reached 21) ]
